cartridge "THE FORTRESS OF ADNIL/GEORGE W. MILLER, COMPUTE!/1983"

' THE FORTRESS OF ADNIL
' The game by George W. Miller from COMPUTE!, July 1983 (pages 92-97),
' for the Timex/Sinclair 1000 (the ZX81) with 16K, for the ColecoVision in
' Amy by Amy Bienvenu. Ported by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic) for Amy
' Studio, 2026.
'
' Source: the listing, transcribed in orig/adnil-zx81.bas from the scans
' beside it. Labels such as l250 are its line numbers.
'
' The game: you are a magician-warrior (+) in Adnil's fortress, a field of
' barricades. Every move costs a unit of energy (you start with 50, shown
' in the bottom border); energy pellets (inverse *) give 10, the treasure
' (inverse $) 200, and a new treasure appears now and then. With each move
' Adnil places two more NAWS (black squares) at random: step on one, or
' have one land on you, and you are captured: with more than 1000 units
' you pay a ransom of 1000, otherwise the quest ends. The laser sword
' reaches five cells: it cuts a barricade, burns a treasure in its path,
' costs 5 units a cell when it meets a pellet, and fights a NAW at even
' odds (won: +100 and the NAW is gone; lost: the beam comes back and
' destroys you). Over 2000 units and you have defeated Adnil.
'
' Controls (either joystick): the stick moves (the ZX81's 5, 6, 7 and 8
' keys); the button is the laser sword (the 9 key): hold it, then push the
' stick the way to strike. A diagonal is no key, as two keys were on the
' ZX81. The button answers the pages ("PRESS ANY KEY").
'
' The ZX81's screen is kept cell for cell: its display file (33 bytes a
' row with the NEWLINE) lives in VRAM at $1C00 and the game PEEKs and
' POKEs it with the listing's S offsets (zx.asm). Each cell is drawn 7
' pixels wide, so the 32 columns fit in ColecoVision columns 2-29 (art.py
' explains); the 22 rows are screen rows 1-22. Black on white, as on the
' ZX81.
'
' Liberties taken:
' - Timing: a POKE of the blinking + is 4 frames; each NAW placed 2; a
'   step of the laser's beam 3 for the dash and 1 for the blank; PAUSE n is
'   n frames (the TS1000 is a 60 Hz machine) and, as on the ZX81, ends
'   early at a key: here a new press of the button.
' - The stick is read every frame while you blink, not only at line 280.
' - Cells the ZX81 could not have held you in: the listing lets you step
'   onto the energy's digits in the bottom border, from there further down
'   would POKE past the display file and left onto row 20's NEWLINE (a
'   crash on the ZX81); here a step or a beam off the display file or onto
'   a NEWLINE is stopped like the border.
' - Line 7500's message is 33 characters (one too many for the row: the
'   ZX81 would stop with an error); its last space is dropped.
' - The pages name the stick and the button instead of the keys, and
'   "PRESS ANY KEY" reads "PRESS THE BUTTON"; "DO YOU NEED INSTRUCTIONS?"
'   is answered by choosing Y or N with the stick and the button.
' - Sounds (the ZX81 is silent); a credits page with an original tune
'   ("The King of Annep") played once; the font is the 5 x 7 font of
'   games/hs, the ZX81's block graphics drawn after its character set.
' Quirks kept: the beam's line 2080 can never happen (it tests a NAW
' already dealt with), so cutting a barricade costs only the move; a beam
' burns any treasure in its path; C counts the pellets taken and, while
' it is 15, every move scatters 20 more pellets and skips the rest of the
' move (no cost, the cell you left keeps a +, a NAW there does not catch
' you) until another pellet makes it 16; a NAW can land on you, or on
' a barricade you walk into; line 580 draws RND every move (the ZX81's AND evaluates both sides).
'
' House rules kept: joystick only; all text within columns 2-29 and rows
' 1-22; every screen drawn with the NMI off after a wait.

' the ZX81 codes the listing PEEKs and POKEs
const ZBar = 136
const ZNaw = 128
const ZGold = 141
const ZPill = 151
const ZYou = 149
const ZYou2 = 21
const ZLaser = 22
const ZNl = 118
const ZDash = 22
const SndHush = 1
const SndStep = 2
const SndPill = 3
const SndGold = 4
const SndZap = 5
const SndBeat = 6
const SndCaught = 7
const SndRansom = 8
const SndBoom = 9
const SndTuneA = 10
const SndTuneB = 11

' --- the listing's variables -----------------------------------------------
' S your cell, P the cell you left, B the beam's cell (offsets from D_FILE),
' T the energy (the score), C the pellets taken, Z the moves since a
' treasure, G 1 once play has begun, X Y the random row and column (GOSUB
' 1000), N the beam's step, Kc the key (A$: 5 6 7 8 9, 0 none), Bd the
' beam's way (B$)
u16 S = 347
u16 P = 347
u16 B = 0
i16 T = 50
u8 C = 0
u8 Z = 0
u8 G = 0
u8 X = 1
u8 Y = 1
u8 N = 0
u8 Nn = 0
u8 Kc = 0
u8 Kd = 0
u8 Bd = 0
u8 Cd = 0
u8 Dead = 0
' Yc: what your cell showed when the key came (149 or 21; for the bot)
u8 Yc = 0

' --- the display file (zx.asm) ---------------------------------------------
u16 ZS = 0
u8 ZC = 0
u8 ZRow = 0
u8 ZCol = 0
u8 ZX7 = 0
u8 ZCc = 0
u8 ZOf = 0
u8 ZLn = 0
u16 ZRb = 0
u8 ZBuf[8]

' --- random numbers (AdRnd: Rs, ZQ = INT(RND * ZN)) ------------------------
u16 Rs = $5A3C
u8 ZN = 1
u8 ZQ = 0

' --- for the test bot ------------------------------------------------------
' Ph: 1 blinking (waiting for a key), 7 the laser waiting for its way,
' 3 a move, 2 a new fortress, 4 captured or ransom, 5 the end, 8 the end's
' PRESS THE BUTTON, 6 the pages; Mv the moves done; Rs0 Rs at line 10
u8 Ph = 6
u16 Mv = 0
u16 Rs0 = 0
' Pn: pages shown (for the bot)
u8 Pn = 0

u16 Tn = 0
u8 Dg[6]
u8 Nd = 0
u8 V = 0
u8 W = 0
u8 J8 = 0
u8 Kp = 0
u8 Rel = 0
u8 Hit = 0
u16 Fr = 0
u16 V16 = 0
u8 Ans = 0

' visible attribution: THE FORTRESS OF ADNIL BY GEORGE W. MILLER, FROM COMPUTE! JULY 1983

